11. How do you configure Jest to run tests in parallel?
-> To configure Jest to run tests in parallel, you need to set the maxWorkers option in the Jest configuration. This option specifies the maximum number of workers the Jest CLI will spawn when running tests. By default, Jest runs tests serially in the same process (without spawning workers).

For example, if you want to run tests in parallel with 4 workers, you can add the following to your package.json file:
"jest": { "maxWorkers": 4 }
Or you can run Jest with the following command line argument:
jest --maxWorkers=4

12. What is jest.fn() and how is it different from mocking a module?
-> 
🔹 jest.fn() creates a mock function to track how a function is used.
🔹 jest.mock() is used to mock an entire module or library (like axios, react-router-dom, etc.)

13. How do you test a functional component that fetches data?
-> 
📦 You mock the API (e.g., using jest.mock or msw)
🕒 Then use findBy... or waitFor to wait for the DOM to update after fetch.

const mockData = [{ name: "React" }];
jest.mock("./api", () => ({
  fetchData: jest.fn(() => Promise.resolve(mockData)),
}));

await waitFor(() => {
  expect(screen.getByText("React")).toBeInTheDocument();
});

14. What's the difference between getBy, queryBy, and findBy?
-> 
getBy: check for required element
queryBy: check if element doesn’t exist
findBy: wait for element after async change

Query           If NOT found                 Async?           Use case
getBy❌         throws error❌              No               element MUST exist
queryBy✅       returns null❌              No               element may NOT exist
findBy❌        throws after timeout✅      Yes              element appears later

